{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in those many days</b> — Scripture does not say \"in those days\" and \"on that day\" except about a standing time whose event it mentions, but after the fact it says \"and it came to pass after that.\" And the fitting expression here would be: \"and it came to pass after many days, and the king of Egypt died.\" Therefore our Rabbis said (Exodus Rabbah 1:41) that because they were days of distress, he calls them \"many,\" and he alludes to the present time. And so they said (there 1:32) concerning the verse stated above, \"And it came to pass in those days, and Moses grew up\" (Exodus 2:11), that he grew in a manner contrary to the way of the world — that is, in a single period, swiftly. Nevertheless, \"in those days\" from the day the king of Egypt died and the children of Israel cried out — <b>and their cry came up to Elohim</b> — were but a few days. Rather, we shall explain that when he says \"in those days,\" he means the days of affliction and hard labor, which were very many days, for the exile was exceedingly long. And this, that they cried out — <b>and their cry came up</b> — is like \"And it came to pass after many days, and the word of YHWH came to Elijah in the third year\" (1 Kings 18:1), where there were many days and this occurred during them.\n\nIn my opinion, the intent of this verse alludes to the days when Moses was fleeing from Pharaoh. For in truth he fled in his youth: Scripture says, \"And Moses grew up and went out to his brethren,\" and this was immediately when he grew up and reached understanding, and they told him that he was a Jew, and he yearned to see the burdens of his brethren and their toil and their oppression. And on the day he went out he struck the Egyptian, and on the second day they informed on him and he fled. He was then about twelve years old, as our Rabbies mentioned (Exodus Rabbah 5:2), and in any case he had not reached twenty. And when he stood before Pharaoh he was eighty years old. If so, he stood some sixty years fleeing from Pharaoh.\n\nIt is likely that at the end of that period he came to Midian and married Zipporah, for when this divine speech came to him, he had not yet begotten from her any son except his firstborn Gershom. But Scripture did not mention in the account of his flight (Exodus 2:15) anything except \"and he dwelt in the land of Midian and sat by the well,\" because nothing new happened in those later days that Scripture needed to relate. And so it was: one who flees from the kingdom will not tarry in a settled city nearby, but will flee from place to place, to a distant land. And so he stood many days hiding and concealing himself from nation to nation and from kingdom to another people, and at the end of the time he came to Midian and tarried there. And this is the meaning of \"and he dwelt in the land of Midian,\" for it should have said \"and he went to the land of Midian,\" but it hinted that he did not settle in a city until the end, when he came to the land of Midian and there he settled.\n\nAnd because Scripture mentioned his flight from Pharaoh and his dwelling in Midian, and then comes to mention immediately that he was returned to Egypt by command of the Holy One, blessed be He, it would have appeared that all these events mentioned were done immediately and pressed one upon another within a single year. Therefore Scripture came to allude to us the matter as it was, and said <b>and it came to pass in those many days</b> — that Moses was fleeing from Pharaoh and standing in Midian throughout them, and during those days, at their end, the king of Egypt died, and there was a revelation of the Shekhinah to Moses concerning this matter, and he was returned to Egypt and redeemed them. And therefore it did not say \"and it came to pass after many days,\" for that would have implied from the time Moses settled in Midian, and those many days were not except for all that was recounted above." "<b>And the king of Egypt died, and they groaned</b> — he was stricken with leprosy and would slaughter Israelite infants and bathe in their blood. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 2:23). And it is a midrash of the Sages (Exodus Rabbah 1:41). And according to the plain sense: it is the way of all slaves under a wicked and worthless king that they look forward and wait for the day of his death, and when they saw that the king had died, they groaned exceedingly because a wicked and profane man was enthroned in place of the first, for they said: our hope is lost, it is decreed upon us, and they chose death over life. And this is the meaning of \"their groaning,\" for they groaned the groan of the slain (cf. Ezekiel 30:24)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:39.610942535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}